\section{Experiments}

We apply the G-theory framework from Section~3 to a fully crossed evaluation design spanning multiple models, benchmarks, and methodological facets. All experiments use open-weight 7--9B parameter models evaluated with vLLM~0.9.2 on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96GB). Binary correctness (1/0) serves as the primary outcome measure throughout; generation-based scoring avoids the log-likelihood artifacts identified by \citet{hua2025flaw}.

\subsection{Single-Model Baseline}

We first establish the variance structure within a single model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) on MMLU to isolate methodological facet contributions before introducing cross-model variation.

\paragraph{Design.} The G-study crosses six facets: 200 items $\times$ 3 numerical precisions (BF16, FP16, FP32) $\times$ 6 prompt templates $\times$ 6 random seeds $\times$ 3 temperature levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) $\times$ 4 answer orderings, yielding 239,200 binary observations. Variance components are estimated via Henderson Method~I with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples).

\paragraph{Results.} Under binary scoring, item difficulty (\texttt{item\_id}) accounts for 61.1\% of total variance, with the residual term contributing 19.5\%. All main facet effects are negligible ($<$1\% each): prompt template 0.3\%, temperature 0.2\%, precision 0.1\%, seed $<$0.1\%, ordering $<$0.1\%. The interesting structure appears in item-conditional interactions: precision$\times$item contributes 7.2\%, temperature$\times$item 6.9\%, and prompt$\times$item 5.7\%. This pattern indicates that methodological choices do not shift performance uniformly across items; instead, specific items are differentially sensitive to specific facets.

The resulting generalizability coefficient is $G_\text{item} = 0.918$, indicating that 200 items under multi-facet conditions produce highly reliable rankings. A D-study projection shows $G \geq 0.80$ is achievable with as few as 72 items in this multi-facet design.

For text-exact-match scoring (requiring verbatim string matches), the variance structure shifts dramatically: temperature dominates at 79.7\% of variance, and $G_\text{item}$ collapses to 0.007. This metric-conditional divergence motivates our exclusive use of binary correctness for the remaining analyses.

\subsection{Cross-Model Variance}

We extend the single-model design to four models: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Gemma-2-9B-it, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3, and Qwen3-8B. A balanced BF16 subset (345,600 records) serves as the primary analysis sample, chosen because BF16 is the standard inference precision for these architectures.

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} presents the variance decomposition. Introducing model as a facet reshapes the structure: item difficulty drops from 61.1\% to 42.1\% [36.0--48.8\%], while model$\times$item interaction emerges as the second-largest component at 33.6\% [28.4--37.9\%]. The model main effect contributes only 0.7\%, confirming that models differ not in overall ability level but in \emph{which items} they find difficult.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Variance decomposition for the cross-model MMLU design (4 models, BF16, 345,600 records). Components estimated via Henderson Method~I with 1,000-resample bootstrap CIs. $G_\text{item} = 0.820$ [0.782--0.861].}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrl}
\toprule
Component & Var.\% & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{42.1} & [36.0--48.8] \\
\underline{model$\times$item} & \underline{33.6} & [28.4--37.9] \\
residual & 19.5 & [17.2--21.8] \\
precision$\times$item & 1.3 & [0.8--1.9] \\
model & 0.7 & [0.2--1.5] \\
prompt$\times$item & 0.6 & [0.3--1.0] \\
temperature$\times$item & 0.6 & [0.3--1.0] \\
seed$\times$item & 0.4 & [0.1--0.8] \\
ordering$\times$item & 0.3 & [0.1--0.6] \\
all others & $<$0.1 & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The per-model decomposition of interaction magnitude reveals meaningful asymmetry: Mistral concentrates 29.3\% of the total model$\times$item variance, followed by Qwen (27.1\%), Gemma (24.0\%), and Llama (19.7\%). This ordering suggests that instruction-tuning strategies and pretraining data composition produce model-specific item difficulty profiles.

\paragraph{D-study validation.} To test whether G-theory projections generalize beyond the training sample, we estimate a D-study on 3-model subsets and predict the 4-model coefficient. The predicted $G = 0.816$ for $n_\text{model} = 4$ closely matches the observed $G = 0.820$ ($\Delta = 0.004$). Forward projections indicate $G = 0.848$ at 5 models, $G = 0.893$ at 8 models, and $G \geq 0.90$ requires approximately 10 models.

\subsection{Format-Conditional Variance Structure}
\label{sec:format_conditional}

The preceding analyses use only MMLU, a multiple-choice benchmark. We now test whether the item-dominated variance structure generalizes across evaluation formats by extending to four benchmarks spanning two response formats.

\paragraph{Design.} The expanded design (exp-002) crosses 4 models $\times$ 4 benchmarks $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures (0.0, 0.7) $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items per benchmark, totaling 921,600 records. The benchmarks include three multiple-choice tasks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag) and one free-form generation task (GSM8K). The ordering facet operates only on MMLU, permuting few-shot demonstration order. For other benchmarks, ordering is structurally inert ($\sigma^2 = 0$ by construction) and retained for design balance.

% [PLACEHOLDER: expand to 8 models × 5 benchmarks (+ MATH) when data ready]

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} reveals a qualitative structural shift between formats. For all three multiple-choice benchmarks, item difficulty is the dominant component (MMLU 42\%, ARC 49\%, HellaSwag 45\%), and model$\times$item interaction is the second largest (32--38\%). This structure is consistent with the cross-model analysis in Section~4.2.

GSM8K exhibits a fundamentally different variance profile. Item difficulty accounts for only 12\% of variance. Instead, model (15\%), prompt template (12\%), and model$\times$prompt interaction (9\%) become the leading components. The residual term absorbs 35\% of variance, indicating substantial unexplained variation from seed and temperature effects in free-form generation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Format-conditional variance structure across four benchmarks (exp-002, 921,600 records). Top-5 components plus residual per benchmark. MC = multiple-choice, FF = free-form. Bootstrap CIs omitted for compactness; all MC item\_id shares have 95\% CIs within $\pm$6 pp.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\small
\begin{tabular}{l rrrr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MC} & FF \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-5}
Component & MMLU & ARC & Hella. & GSM8K \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{42} & \textbf{49} & \textbf{45} & 12 \\
model$\times$item & 34 & 32 & 38 & 8 \\
\textbf{model} & 1 & 1 & 1 & \textbf{15} \\
prompt & $<$1 & $<$1 & $<$1 & 12 \\
model$\times$prompt & $<$1 & $<$1 & $<$1 & 9 \\
residual & 19 & 15 & 13 & 35 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
% [PLACEHOLDER: add MATH column + 8-model rows when data ready]
\end{table}

This dichotomy is not a continuous gradient. The gap between the lowest MC item dominance (MMLU, 42\%) and GSM8K (12\%) represents a qualitative shift in what drives evaluation variance. In multiple-choice settings, the item pool determines reliability; in free-form settings, prompt design and model selection dominate. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes this structural contrast.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_cross_benchmark}
\caption{Variance decomposition by benchmark format. Multiple-choice benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) share an item-dominated structure, while free-form GSM8K distributes variance across model, prompt, and interaction components.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Precision Separability}

Prior work has identified numerical precision as a potential source of evaluation variance \cite{yuan2025layercast}. Our multi-facet design allows us to isolate the precision contribution while controlling for all other facets simultaneously.

Under binary scoring with BF16 and FP16, the mean accuracy difference is 0.23 percentage points. After excluding FP32, the precision main effect drops to 0.0006\% of total variance. The FP32 anomaly traces to a vLLM implementation detail (\texttt{enforce\_eager} mode required for FP32 inference), not a fundamental precision effect.

Precision does not interact with other methodological facets under binary scoring: all precision$\times$methodology interactions contribute $<$0.1\% of variance. Under text-exact-match scoring, however, a precision$\times$temperature interaction emerges at 1.3\%, consistent with the metric-conditional pattern observed in Section~4.1. The practical implication is clear: for standard binary evaluation at BF16/FP16, numerical precision is a negligible variance source that need not be controlled. This finding is specific to single-GPU inference and does not address multi-GPU tensor parallelism, where numerical non-associativity introduces additional variance sources \cite{shanmugavelu2024floating}.

\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

The previous sections establish that item selection and model$\times$item interaction dominate evaluation variance. A natural question follows: how many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We address this through D-study projections that translate variance components into concrete item budgets.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares four evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold. The multi-facet design (432 conditions per item: 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 3 temperatures $\times$ 4 orderings) achieves $G = 0.918$ at 200 items and requires only 72 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design (one fixed prompt, seed, ordering, and precision per item) reaches only $G = 0.559$ at 200 items and requires 631 items for the same threshold. The ratio between these budgets is 8.8$\times$, and this ratio remains stable across subset sizes from 50 to 200 items (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs. The multi-facet design requires $8.8\times$ fewer items than single-condition evaluation to reach $G \geq 0.80$.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcrrr}
\toprule
Design & Cond./item & $n_{G \geq 0.80}$ & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 432 & \textbf{72} & \textbf{0.918} & \textbf{0.882} \\
Partial (4 cond.) & 4 & 189 & 0.810 & 0.721 \\
Single-condition & 1 & 631 & 0.559 & 0.424 \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & \multicolumn{1}{c}{---} & 116 & --- & 0.424$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT item count of 116 \cite{zhou2026irt} evaluated under single-condition protocol yields $G = 0.424$. See text for interpretation.}
\end{table}

The comparison with IRT-based efficient benchmarking is instructive. \citet{zhou2026irt} use IRT to select 116 informative items from MMLU, optimizing for item discrimination. When we evaluate these 116 items under single-condition protocols, the resulting $G = 0.424$ falls well below the reliability threshold. This is not a failure of IRT item selection; rather, it reflects that IRT optimizes a different objective (latent trait estimation) that does not account for methodological facet variance. IRT selects items with high trait discrimination; multi-facet G-study improves reliability orthogonally by averaging over methodological noise. Under the multi-facet design, 116 items yield $G = 0.882$, confirming that the reliability gap is driven by evaluation design, not item quality.

Figure~\ref{fig:dstudy} plots the D-study projection curves for each design. The multi-facet curve saturates rapidly (diminishing returns beyond $\sim$150 items), while the single-condition curve rises slowly and does not reach $G = 0.80$ within typical benchmark sizes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_dstudy_curves}
\caption{D-study projection curves for three evaluation designs. The multi-facet design ($8.8\times$ fewer items) reaches $G \geq 0.80$ at 72 items; the single-condition design requires 631 items. The shaded band shows 95\% bootstrap CIs.}
\label{fig:dstudy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Robustness Checks}

We conduct six robustness analyses to verify that the reported variance structures are not artifacts of specific analytical choices. \textbf{Item resampling} (exp-001b): three independent 200-item samples drawn from the full MMLU pool and evaluated under a BF16-only 5-facet design yield item difficulty variance shares of 75.9\%, 75.4\%, and 72.2\% (CV = 2.7\%) and $G_\text{item}$ values with CV = 0.5\%. The higher item share relative to the 6-facet baseline (61.1\%) reflects the absence of the precision facet and its item interactions. \textbf{Estimator robustness}: REML and Henderson Method~I differ by $<$0.5 percentage points on all components. \textbf{CI method}: bootstrap and jackknife confidence intervals agree closely (width ratio 0.96--1.04 across components). \textbf{Facet specification}: treating facets as fixed vs.\ random changes $G_\text{item}$ by only 0.37 percentage points. \textbf{Leave-one-model-out (LOMO)}: all three-model subsets preserve the item + model$\times$item dominance pattern (item\_id 39--45\%, model$\times$item 32--37\%, $G_\text{item}$ 0.747--0.793). \textbf{Null calibration}: a Bernoulli simulation with matched item difficulties produces a variance ratio of 1.0008 relative to observed data, confirming that the decomposition does not introduce systematic bias on binary outcomes.

% [PLACEHOLDER: §4.7 Extended Results — 8-model × 5-benchmark expansion]

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Generalizability Theory}
\label{sec:gtheory}

Generalizability theory (G-theory) treats each measurement as one observation from a universe of admissible conditions defined by the crossed facets of an evaluation design \cite{shavelson1991generalizability, brennan2001generalizability}. The \emph{object of measurement} is the entity whose consistency is of interest. In our setting, test items ($p$) serve as the object of measurement, and the goal is to determine how reliably item-level scores generalize across the methodological conditions under which they are collected.

A \emph{G-study} decomposes total observed variance into components $\sigma^2_x$ for each facet $x$ and their interactions. Each component quantifies how much variability that source contributes to the overall measurement. Large interaction components (e.g., $\sigma^2_{pM}$ for item-by-model) indicate that facets do not affect items uniformly but instead create differential difficulty profiles. We estimate variance components via Henderson Method~I, an unbiased ANOVA-based estimator for balanced designs \cite[Ch.~3]{brennan2001generalizability}, with 1,000-resample bootstrap confidence intervals \cite{li2023bootstrap}.

A \emph{D-study} projects the generalizability coefficient under hypothetical designs with different numbers of conditions per facet:
\begin{equation}
G_\text{item} = \frac{\sigma^2_p}{\sigma^2_p + \sigma^2_\text{rel}}
\label{eq:gcoeff}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma^2_\text{rel}$ aggregates all item-by-facet interaction variances, each divided by the number of conditions for the corresponding facets \cite[Ch.~4]{brennan2001generalizability}. This prospective capability distinguishes G-theory from classical ANOVA: rather than only attributing variance post hoc, a D-study enables optimization of evaluation designs \emph{before} data collection \cite{shavelson1989gtheory}. Throughout this paper, we use the following notation: $p$ = item, $M$ = model, $\pi$ = numerical precision, $\delta$ = prompt template, $s$ = random seed, $\tau$ = sampling temperature, $o$ = answer ordering, and $\sigma^2_x$ = variance component for source $x$.

\subsection{Experimental Design: MMLU}
\label{sec:exp001}

We design a fully crossed factorial experiment (exp-001) that simultaneously varies seven facets of LLM evaluation on MMLU. Table~\ref{tab:design_matrix} summarizes the complete design matrix, and Figure~\ref{fig:design_diagram} illustrates the crossing structure.

\paragraph{Facets.} Items ($p$): 200 MMLU questions stratified across 10 subjects (20 per subject). The stratified sampling ensures that variance estimates are not dominated by a single knowledge domain; the 10 subjects span STEM, humanities, social sciences, and professional disciplines. Models ($M$): Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Gemma-2-9B-it, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3, and Qwen3-8B, a purposive sample from four major architecture families treated as a quasi-random facet (\S3.6). Numerical precision ($\pi$): BF16, FP16, and FP32, all evaluated with \texttt{enforce\_eager=True} for fair comparison across precision levels. Prompt template ($\delta$): six semantically equivalent paraphrases of the evaluation instruction \cite{sclar2024formatting}. Random seed ($s$): six fixed values (42, 123, 456, 789, 1024, 2048). Sampling temperature ($\tau$): three levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) spanning deterministic to moderately stochastic decoding. Answer ordering ($o$): four cyclic rotations of multiple-choice option positions \cite{pezeshkpour2024ordering}.

\paragraph{Sample sizes.} The cross-model BF16 balanced subset yields 345,600 records (4 models $\times$ 200 items $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 3 temperatures $\times$ 4 orderings). A single-model analysis (Llama) spans all three precisions for 239,200 records (1,196 conditions $\times$ 200 items; 100 of the theoretical 1,296 conditions were excluded due to vLLM generation failures under specific precision$\times$temperature$\times$seed combinations). The BF16 subset serves as the primary cross-model sample because BF16 is the standard inference precision for these architectures. Gemma-2 does not support FP16 due to vLLM numerical instability guards; cross-model analyses therefore use BF16 to maintain a balanced design.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fully crossed G-theory design. Exp-001 evaluates MMLU across all facet levels; exp-002 extends to four benchmarks with reduced crossing. $^\dagger$BF16 balanced subset for cross-model analysis.}
\label{tab:design_matrix}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Facet & Levels & exp-001 & exp-002 \\
\midrule
Item ($p$) & Stratified sample & 200 & 200$\times$4 \\
Model ($M$) & 4 architecture families & 4 & 4 \\
Precision ($\pi$) & BF16 / FP16 / FP32 & 3 & 1 \\
Prompt ($\delta$) & Paraphrases & 6 & 6 \\
Seed ($s$) & Fixed set & 6 & 6 \\
Temperature ($\tau$) & 0.0 / 0.3 / 0.7 & 3 & 2 \\
Ordering ($o$) & Cyclic rotations & 4 & 4 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Total records} & 345,600$^\dagger$ & 921,600 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_design_diagram}
\caption{Fully crossed G-theory design for LLM evaluation. Each observation is a binary correctness score determined by the crossing of seven facets: item ($p$), model ($M$), precision ($\pi$), prompt ($\delta$), seed ($s$), temperature ($\tau$), and ordering ($o$).}
\label{fig:design_diagram}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Extension}
\label{sec:exp002}

To test whether the variance structure observed on MMLU generalizes across evaluation formats, exp-002 extends the design to four benchmarks: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, and HellaSwag (multiple-choice), and GSM8K (free-form generation). The design crosses 4 models $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures (0.0, 0.7) $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items per benchmark, totaling 921,600 records. This reduced crossing fixes precision at BF16 and uses two temperature levels to concentrate statistical power on format-conditional differences. The ordering facet operates only on MMLU, permuting few-shot demonstration order. For other benchmarks, ordering is structurally inert ($\sigma^2 = 0$ by construction) and retained for design balance. The two temperature levels bracket the most common evaluation configurations: deterministic ($\tau = 0.0$) and a moderate stochastic setting ($\tau = 0.7$) representative of practical deployment.

% [PLACEHOLDER: 8 models × 5 benchmarks when expansion complete]

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

All evaluations use generation-based scoring: models produce free-text responses from which correctness is determined by answer extraction, rather than by ranking answer options via token log-likelihood. This design choice follows \citet{hua2025flaw}, who show that log-likelihood scoring conflates model confidence with surface-level token biases. We report two metrics: binary correctness (primary, 1/0 per item) and text-exact-match (secondary). Binary correctness accepts any response containing the correct answer letter or numerical value after standard normalization; text-exact-match requires the generated string to match the reference answer character-for-character.

Inference runs on vLLM~0.9.2 with default settings and without batch-invariant kernels. This configuration reflects standard practitioner conditions and preserves CUDA-level nondeterminism from floating-point non-associativity as a measurable variance source \cite{messina2026background, thinkingmachines2025batch}. Few-shot prompting follows standard protocols: 5-shot for MMLU and ARC-Challenge, 8-shot for GSM8K, and 10-shot for HellaSwag.

\subsection{Binary Data Assumptions}
\label{sec:binary}

Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal outcome data \cite{shavelson1991generalizability}. Our binary correctness scores (0/1) violate this assumption. We establish robustness through four complementary analyses.

\paragraph{Simulation.} We generate continuous latent scores from a probit model matched to our 1,296-condition design, then dichotomize at the empirical accuracy threshold. Spearman rank correlation between binary and continuous variance component rankings is $\rho = 0.732$ (range 0.716--0.751 across accuracy levels), confirming that Henderson Method~I recovers the relative ordering of variance sources despite binary data. Henderson Method~I identifies \texttt{item\_id} as the dominant variance source in all 100 simulation replications.

\paragraph{GLMM complement.} A generalized linear mixed model (GLMM) with marginal logit link produces variance component rankings that perfectly preserve all seven grouped component orderings ($\rho = 1.0$) relative to Henderson Method~I \cite{jiang2018bayesian, tuerlinckx2006glmm}.

\paragraph{Accuracy-range stability.} The \texttt{item\_id} variance share remains stable across subsets with mean accuracy from 0.62 to 0.72 ($r = -0.048$). This stability rules out distortion from binary floor or ceiling effects at observed accuracy levels.

\paragraph{Estimator guarantee.} Henderson Method~I produces unbiased variance component estimates for balanced designs regardless of the outcome distribution \cite[Ch.~12]{brennan2001generalizability}. Our fully crossed design satisfies this balance condition.

\subsection{Model-as-Facet Justification}
\label{sec:model_facet}

Treating four purposively selected models as a quasi-random facet requires empirical validation. We provide three forms of evidence.

D-study cross-validation: variance components estimated from all $\binom{4}{3} = 4$ three-model subsets predict the four-model generalizability coefficient. The predicted $G = 0.816$ for $n_\text{model} = 4$ closely matches the observed $G = 0.820$ ($\Delta = 0.004$). This agreement confirms that the model facet behaves as expected under random-effects assumptions.

Leave-one-model-out (LOMO) stability: all four three-model subsets preserve \texttt{item\_id} as the dominant component and rank \texttt{model$\times$item} as the second-largest ($G_\text{item}$ range: 0.747--0.793). The consistency of rankings across all subsets indicates that no single model disproportionately drives the observed variance structure.

Forward projection: D-study estimates predict $G = 0.893$ at eight models. This result supports the quasi-random facet assumption for the broader population of instruction-tuned 7--9B architectures.

% [PLACEHOLDER: 8-model expansion strengthens to ~0.893 observed]

